<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead"><strong>The serve must be below 1.15 metres - unless your competition is running the other law, in which case it is below your waist.</strong> BWF publishes both at once, deliberately. Law 9.1.6 sets the fixed height; the Alternative Service Laws keep the old waist-and-lowest-rib test plus the downward-pointing racket, and referees are instructed to tell team managers which set is in use. So the clubmate insisting it is about your waist is not behind the times. In English tournaments they are quoting the default.</p>

      <p>You know how this goes: someone serves, someone else says "that was above your waist", and the rally stops while four adults who have played for years work out that not one of them can quote the rule.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:30px">
      <h4>The short version</h4>
      <ul style="margin:10px 0 0">
        <li><strong>There are two service-height laws, both current.</strong> 1.15 metres, or the waist and lowest rib with the racket pointing down. In English tournaments the waist rule is the <em>default</em>, and the fixed height is what a tournament has to opt into.</li>
        <li><strong>The fixed height comes with a measuring device.</strong> Referees are told to check umpires can use one. No device beside the court is a strong hint about which law you are under.</li>
        <li><strong>The receiving side never swaps service courts.</strong> Law 11.1.3, and it is what decides who receives next.</li>
        <li><strong>Served from the wrong court and won the rally? Keep the point.</strong> Law 12.2: correct the error, the existing score stands. No let, no replay, no penalty.</li>
        <li><strong>A serve that clips the net and lands in is good.</strong> There is no service let for a net cord. Caught on the tape or in the net is a fault, not a let.</li>
        <li><strong>You cannot put spin on the shuttle as you release it.</strong> Law 9.1.5, and it is recent enough that most guides still describe the older rule.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:22px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem">Written for UK club-level doubles. Law numbering follows the BWF Laws of Badminton in force 26 April 2025, and the Alternative Service Laws at Section 4.1.4 of the BWF Statutes. Para badminton has its own service provisions, noted where they apply.</p>

    <div class="guide-toc" style="margin-top:26px">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#height">How high can you hit the serve?</a>
      <a href="#who-serves">Who serves next, and who stands where?</a>
      <a href="#wrong-court">Wrong service court, and you won the rally</a>
      <a href="#net-cord">The serve clips the net</a>
      <a href="#feint">Pauses, feints and wind-ups</a>
