    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">

      <h2 id="height">How high can you hit the serve?</h2>
      <p>The shuttle must be below 1.15 metres from the floor at the instant your racket hits it - or below your waist, if your competition is running the alternative law.</p>
      <p>Both are live. The main Laws of Badminton give the fixed height at Law 9.1.6: the whole shuttle below 1.15 metres from the surface of the court at the instant of being hit.</p>
      <p>Separately, BWF maintains a short document of Alternative Laws, and its service section replaces 9.1.6 with two clauses familiar to anyone who learned the game before 2018. The shuttle must be below the server's waist, defined as an imaginary line round the body level with the lowest part of the bottom rib. And the shaft and racket head must point downward at the instant of hitting.</p>
      <p>That second clause is the half everybody forgets. The old rule was always two tests, not one, and the racket angle was the part that stopped players driving a serve flat from below waist height.</p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/service-height-two-laws.svg" alt="Two servers of different heights shown against both service-height laws at once. A single horizontal line at 1.15 metres crosses both of them at the same height above the floor, with a service height measuring device standing beside the court, because the fixed-height law does not vary between players. A separate marker on each player shows the lowest rib, which sits at a different height above the floor on each of them because the alternative law measures from the body rather than from the floor. Both players have the racket shaft and head angled downward, as the alternative law also requires. Rib heights are schematic and vary between players." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">The same serve, judged two ways. The fixed height is the same for everyone on court. The waist line moves with the player, which is exactly why it was replaced - and exactly why it is easier to judge without equipment.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p style="margin-top:22px"><strong>Which one applies is a decision someone makes before the tournament starts.</strong> BWF's instructions to technical officials tell the Referee to remind team managers whether the fixed height service or the alternative service laws will be in use. They also tell the Referee that if the fixed height law is being used, umpires must be familiar with the service height measuring device and given training if they need it.</p>
      <p>That last instruction is the practical tell, and it explains a great deal. The fixed height is tied to a piece of equipment: a measuring device set at 1.15 metres beside the court, which the instructions require umpires to be trained on and the service judge to confirm is in place and level. Where there is no device, judging a fixed height by eye is guesswork, and the waist test is what a human can actually see.</p>
      <p>Badminton Scotland published exactly that position in guidance dated October 2019: the new law applies only where a service height device is in use beside the court, and at all other tournaments the previous Law, with the waist and the downward racket head, applies. We should flag its age, since it predates both the current Laws edition and the current instructions to officials, but it remains live on their site and nothing we found supersedes it. We could not find an equivalent published position from Badminton England, and we are not going to invent one for them.</p>
      <p>So at your club night, with no device, no service judge and no umpire, you are almost certainly playing something like the waist rule whether anyone has said so or not.</p>
      <p>One clarification, because it is in the Law and it matters to some readers: Para badminton Standing Classes use the Alternative Service Laws as a matter of course under Law 9.1.6.2, and Wheelchair Badminton uses an armpit height under 9.1.6.1 together with the downward-racket clause. Those are not elections. They are the law for those classes.</p>

      <h2 id="who-serves">Who serves next, and who stands where?</h2>
      <p>The serving side swaps courts when it wins a point; the receiving side does not swap at all.</p>
      <p>That single asymmetry is the source of most doubles service confusion, and Law 11.1.3 states it plainly: the player of the receiving side who served last stays in the same service court from where they served last, with the reverse pattern for their partner. Law 11.1.5 adds the general form - players do not change their respective service courts until they win a point when their side is serving.</p>
      <p>Everything else follows from the score. The serving side serves from the right when its score is even, including zero, and from the left when it is odd (Laws 11.1.1 and 11.1.2). The receiver is whoever is standing diagonally opposite (11.1.4).</p>
      <p><strong>And there is only ever one server per turn.</strong> Win the rally while serving and the same player serves again, from the other court (11.3.1). Lose it and the serve passes to the other side (11.3.2). Your partner does not get a go first, and if your club still plays two serves each, that went with the old scoring system.</p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/service-court-rotation.svg" alt="A badminton court from above, shown three times to follow a doubles rally sequence. In the first, the serving side's score is even so their server is in the right service court and the receiver stands diagonally opposite. In the second, the serving side has won the point, so the same server has moved across to the left court and serves again, while both receiving players have stayed exactly where they were. In the third, the receiving side has won the rally and become the serving side, serving from the court that matches their own score, again with no swap by the players who have just become receivers." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">The serving pair swaps courts when it scores. The receiving pair never does. Watch the two players on the right across all three: they do not move between courts once.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h4>Worked example: you are 6-4 up and you have just won the rally</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Your side is serving. It was 5-4, you won the rally, so it is now 6-4.</p>
        <ul style="margin:12px 0 0">
          <li>Your score is <strong>6</strong>, an even number, so this serve goes from the <strong>right</strong> service court (Law 11.1.1).</li>
          <li>You served the last point from the left, because 5 is odd. So you and your partner swap courts and <strong>you serve again</strong>, not your partner (Law 11.3.1).</li>
          <li><strong>Neither opponent moves.</strong> Whoever is now standing diagonally opposite you is the receiver (Laws 11.1.3 and 11.1.4).</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top:12px">Now lose the next rally. The serve passes to the other side at 6-5, they serve from whichever court matches <em>their</em> score, and this time it is <strong>your</strong> pair that stays exactly where it is.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="wrong-court">Wrong service court, and you won the rally</h2>
      <p>Serving from the wrong service court costs you nothing: the error is corrected, and the existing score stands whoever won the rally.</p>
      <p>This surprises almost everyone. A service court error is serving or receiving out of turn, or from the wrong service court (Law 12.1). The remedy, in full, is that the error is corrected when the shuttle is not in play and the existing score stands (Law 12.2). No let, no replay, no point deducted, and it makes no difference who won the rally.</p>
      <p>Club players routinely expect one of three things that the Laws do not provide: replaying the point, awarding it to the other side, or arguing that it depends on who benefited. None of these is in there.</p>

      <h2 id="net-cord">The serve clips the net</h2>
      <p>A serve that clips the net and still lands in the right service court is good. Play on.</p>
      <p>Badminton has no service let for a net cord. The grounds for a let are listed at Law 14.2, running from 14.2.1 to 14.2.7, and a serve that clips the tape and lands in is not among them - though be ready for someone to reach for 14.2.7, "any unforeseen or accidental situation", which a net cord plainly is not. If you have come from tennis this feels wrong, and it is an easy error to import.</p>
      <p><strong>The two ways a net cord does end the rally are both faults, not lets.</strong> A serve caught on the net and left suspended on its top is a fault (13.2.1). A serve that passes over the net and is then caught in the net is also a fault (13.2.2). Either way the serving side loses the point.</p>
      <p>Here is the part worth knowing, because it looks like a contradiction and is not. Those same two events, happening after the service has been returned, are lets (14.2.3). Same shuttle, same net, opposite outcome - the difference is only whether the rally had started. Most clubs play both as lets, which is generous to the server by exactly one point every time.</p>

      <h2 id="feint">Pauses, feints and wind-ups</h2>
      <p>Once your racket has finished moving backwards, any delay in starting the serve is undue delay.</p>
      <p>Law 9.1.2 is unusually blunt about this: on completion of the backward movement of the server's racket head, any delay in the start of the service shall be considered to be an undue delay. Law 9.1.7 then requires that the racket keeps moving forwards from the start of the service until it is delivered. Between them they outlaw the pause-and-restart, the double wind-up and the hold that waits for the receiver to lean.</p>
      <p>What the Laws do not do is put a number on it. There is no permitted delay in seconds anywhere in Law 9, and the general standard in 9.1.1 - neither side shall cause undue delay once both are ready - is umpire judgement. At a club night there is no umpire, so in practice the standard is social. If your serve routine involves a pause that people comment on, it is probably not legal, and you already know it is not.</p>

      <h2 id="spin">Can you put spin on the serve?</h2>
      <p>You cannot add spin to the shuttle as you release it, and this is the newest rule in Law 9.</p>
      <p>Law 9.1.5 requires that the server releases the shuttle without adding spin, and that the racket initially hits the base of the shuttle. The no-spin half ran as an experimental variation until 1 January 2025 and is main-law text in the edition in force from 26 April 2025, which is recent enough that most published guides still describe the older rule.</p>
      <p>One consequence worth stating plainly, because you will read the opposite elsewhere: it is no longer enough to hit the cork first. The release itself must be spin-free.</p>
      <p>The base clause is older and has a longer history: it is what ended the S-serve, where the server struck the feathers to make the shuttle tumble unpredictably. Hitting the base first is what makes a serve's flight readable.</p>

      <h2 id="feet">Where must your feet be?</h2>
      <p>The server and the receiver must both stand inside their own service court without touching a line, with some part of both feet stationary on the floor.</p>
      <p>Two separate requirements, and clubs usually remember one. Law 9.1.3 puts the server and receiver in diagonally opposite service courts without touching the boundary lines of those courts. Touching a line is a fault; there is no tolerance for a toe on the centre line.</p>
      <p>Law 9.1.4 then requires that some part of both feet of the server <em>and the receiver</em> stays in contact with the surface in a stationary position, from the start of the service until it is delivered. That the receiver is bound by this too is the half that gets forgotten. A receiver who is bouncing, stepping in, or lifting a heel as the server starts is faulting, and it is rarely called at a club night.</p>
      <p>What it permits is worth knowing as well. "Some part of both feet" is not "both feet flat and still". You can turn your body, transfer weight, and pivot on the ball of a foot. You cannot drag, lift or step.</p>

      <h2 id="partners">Where can the partners stand?</h2>
      <p>The partners may stand anywhere on their own side of the net, as long as they do not block the opposing server's or receiver's view.</p>
      <p>Law 9.5 gives the partners any positions within their respective courts which do not unsight the opposing server or receiver. Note the word: <em>courts</em>, not service courts. The partners are not confined to a service box, and a partner standing well forward or right across the other half is doing nothing wrong.</p>
      <p><strong>Now the gap.</strong> Law 9.5 forbids unsighting, but Law 13 does not list unsighting as a fault, so nothing in the Laws makes it a point against the player who blocked the view.</p>
      <p>The only mechanism is a let, and even that is a stretch. Any player may call one where there is no umpire (Law 14.1), but the grounds for a let are listed in 14.2 and unsighting is not among them - the nearest is 14.2.7, "any unforeseen or accidental situation". So a player who cannot see the server is relying on goodwill rather than on a rule. In practice clubs either award a fault, which the Laws do not support, or say nothing at all.</p>

      <h2 id="partner-hits">Can the receiver's partner hit the serve?</h2>
      <p>Only the receiver may play the serve: if the receiver's partner hits it, that is a fault against the receiving side (Law 13.2.3).</p>
      <p>This comes up in mixed and in beginners' doubles, usually when a serve drifts towards the wrong player and instinct takes over. It also has a quieter cousin: if the serve touches the receiver's partner without being hit at all, the shuttle has touched the person of a player, which is a fault under 13.3.4. Standing well clear of a serve you are not allowed to touch is the practical lesson.</p>

      <h2 id="miss">You miss the shuttle completely</h2>
      <p>You lose the rally, and there is no second serve.</p>
      <p>Law 9.1.8 is one line: in attempting to serve, the server shall not miss the shuttle. Air-shot the serve and it is a fault. Law 9.3 confirms the logic - the service is delivered when the shuttle is hit by the racket, or when the server misses it. Either way the serve is over.</p>
      <p>The absence is as important as the rule. The Laws never mention a second serve at any point, which is why players arriving from tennis keep expecting one. It is not that badminton took second serves away; it is that they were never in there.</p>

      <h2 id="going-out">The serve was going out and they hit it anyway</h2>
      <p>Once the serve has been returned, the rally is live and where it would have landed stops mattering.</p>
      <p>This one turns on some awkward drafting. Law 9.1 describes a correct service as one where the shuttle's flight is upwards over the net so that, <em>if not intercepted</em>, it shall land in the receiver's service court. Read at speed, that sounds like a serve heading long is a fault even after it has been played.</p>
      <p>The qualifier is doing the work. The Law describes a shuttle that has not been intercepted. Once the receiver has hit it, it has been intercepted, and the question of where it would have gone never arises. If you think a serve is going out, you have to let it drop - which is the same judgement call as any other line, and the same one you will get wrong under pressure.</p>

      <h2 id="not-ready">Serving before the receiver is ready</h2>
      <p>Serving before the receiver is ready is a let rather than a fault, so the rally is replayed instead of the point going to anyone.</p>
      <p>Law 9.4 says the server shall not serve before the receiver is ready, and Law 14.2.1 makes it a let when they do. On a let, play since the last service does not count and the player who served last serves again (Law 14.3).</p>
      <p><strong>But the same Law closes the obvious loophole.</strong> The receiver is considered to have been ready if a return of the service is attempted. You cannot swing at it, miss, and then claim you were not ready. Clubs regularly get this pair backwards, awarding the point to the receiving side when the correct answer is to serve it again.</p>

      <h2 id="next-game">Who serves first in the next game?</h2>
      <p>Either player of the winning pair may serve first in the next game, and either player of the losing pair may receive first.</p>
      <p>Two Laws answer this between them. Law 7.6 gives the serve to the side that won the previous game, and Law 11.6 then leaves it entirely open which of that pair takes it, and which of the losing pair receives. The service order does not carry over, and it is not tied to who served last, who served first last time, or who is standing where.</p>
      <p>Most club players believe the order continues, and it costs nothing to know that it does not. If one of you serves better under pressure, that is now a decision rather than an accident.</p>

      <h2 id="which-law">Which law is your league playing under?</h2>
      <p>Go and read your league's handbook, because that is where the answer lives and it is not the same everywhere.</p>
      <p>BWF's alternative laws exist, and its instructions to officials assume somebody has chosen between them for each tournament. What the Laws do not say is who makes that choice or on what basis. The alternative <em>scoring</em> systems in the same document carry a "by prior arrangement" qualifier; the alternative <em>service</em> laws carry no equivalent. That silence is real, and we are not going to fill it with a guess.</p>
      <p><strong>In England, the answer is published and it is the opposite of what you would guess.</strong> Badminton England's General Regulations for Tournaments 2026 state it in a footnote on the contents page: <em>"The alternative service laws shall be used in all tournaments unless otherwise stated in the terms and conditions of that tournament."</em> The same footnote dates its references to the BWF Laws as at 1 January 2026, so this is a current position rather than an inherited one.</p>
      <p>Read that again, because it settles the argument. The waist-and-lowest-rib test, with the racket pointing down, is not a legacy rule that clubs have failed to update. It is <strong>the default in English domestic tournaments</strong>, and the fixed height is the exception a tournament has to opt into.</p>
      <p>Badminton Scotland arrives at the same place by a different route, tying the fixed height to whether a measuring device is present. Between them, the two published positions we could find both point away from 1.15 metres for ordinary domestic play.</p>
      <p>Below that level, your county or local league publishes its own handbook, and it will either specify the service law, adopt the BWF Laws generally, or say nothing. If it says nothing, the Badminton England default above is the sensible assumption rather than a guess.</p>
      <p><strong>The concession, because this guide would be dishonest without it.</strong> Almost none of this is worth raising on a social club night. Nobody is measuring 1.15 metres, there is no service judge, and a player who starts quoting Law 9.1.4 at a receiver who lifted a heel will not be short of court time because everyone will stop asking them to play.</p>
      <p>The rules worth actually knowing are the four that decide a point already played, where the alternative is an argument nobody can settle: the wrong service court, the net cord, serving before someone was ready, and who serves next. Those come up on their own. The rest is for the night your club plays a league fixture and somebody turns up who does know the difference.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h4>If you only do one thing</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Stop swapping sides when you are receiving. The serving pair changes courts when it scores; the receiving pair never does.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:24px">
        <h4>Fair games, without the admin</h4>
        <p>ePegboard does not umpire anything. What it does is pick the games: balanced pairings on a club night, with court time spread evenly across everyone who turns up, so the evening runs without one person holding a pen and a list. Free for clubs.</p>
