      <h2 id="related">Where to go next</h2>
      <p>The service laws decide who serves and from where. Where everyone stands once the rally is live is a different subject, and a more useful one for most club players: <a href="/guides/badminton-doubles-positioning">badminton doubles positioning</a> covers the two shapes and when to switch between them.</p>
      <p>If you are the person who ends up settling these arguments, you are probably also the person running the night. <a href="/guides/how-to-run-a-badminton-club-night">The complete organiser's checklist</a> covers the rest of it, and <a href="/guides/badminton-club-night-formats-compared">club night formats compared</a> deals with how the games get made in the first place.</p>

      <div style="margin-top:34px;padding-top:22px;border-top:1px solid var(--line)">
        <h4 style="margin:0 0 8px">How this guide was put together</h4>
        <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem;margin:0">ePegboard builds club-night software. We are not umpires and we do not officiate. Every rule here was read from the BWF Laws of Badminton in force 26 April 2025, the Alternative Laws of Badminton at Section 4.1.4 of the BWF Statutes, and BWF's Instructions to Technical Officials, rather than from anyone's summary of them - which matters on this subject, because summaries are where the folklore comes from.</p>
        <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem;margin:12px 0 0">Where we could not establish something, we have said so rather than picking an answer: the Laws themselves do not say who chooses between the two service laws, or on what basis. Badminton England answers it for its own tournaments and we have quoted them; below that level it is your league's to state.</p>
        <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem;margin:12px 0 0"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href="https://extranet.bwf.sport/docs/document-system/81/1466/1470/Section%204.1%20-%20Laws%20of%20Badminton%20-%2026%20April%202025%20V5.0%20(2)%20.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BWF Laws of Badminton, in force 26 April 2025</a> &middot; <a href="https://extranet.bwf.sport/docs/document-system/81/1466/1470/Section%204.1.4%20-%20Alternative%20Laws%20of%20Badminton%20-1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BWF Alternative Laws of Badminton (Section 4.1.4)</a> &middot; <a href="https://extranet.bwf.sport/docs/document-system/81/1466/1470/Section%204.1.1%20-%20Instructions%20to%20Technical%20Officials%20_ITTO_%20-%209%20November%202024%20V7.0%20(1).pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BWF Instructions to Technical Officials</a> &middot; <a href="https://images.gc.badmintonenglandservices.co.uk/53012730-1ee3-11f1-a060-b1b720430faa.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Badminton England, General Regulations for Tournaments 2026 (PDF)</a> &middot; <a href="https://badmintonscotland.org.uk/news/service-height-guidance/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Badminton Scotland service height guidance</a> &middot; <a href="https://bwfbadminton.com/news-single/2017/11/29/experimental-service-law-from-march-2018" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BWF on the experimental service law, November 2017</a></p>
        <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.9rem;margin:12px 0 0">Published 26 August 2026 by ePegboard. Checked against the Laws in force 26 April 2025; the next edition takes effect 4 January 2027 and does not change Law 9.</p>
